#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Year 3 Creative Arts Written Core batch 01: drawing, colour, texture, looking at art, beat w01-l1 through w06-l2 (12)."""
from arts_support import L, pv, create, VIS, MUS

LESSONS = [
L('s2-arts-y3-w01-l1', VIS, ['a line', 'a shape', 'a colour', 'a drawing choice'],
  "Artists build pictures from three basic elements. Lines can be straight, curved, zigzag, thick or thin. Shapes are closed outlines such as circles, squares and triangles, and organic shapes with wobbly edges like leaves. Colour sets the mood: warm colours such as red and orange feel lively, cool colours such as blue and green feel calm. When you look at an artwork, name the line, shape and colour you see, then say what each one does for the picture.",
  "Example: a painting of a stormy sea uses thick zigzag lines for waves, large dark blue triangles for the water and a thin pale yellow curve for the moon. The zigzag lines make the sea feel rough, the dark cool colours add a gloomy mood and the small pale moon looks far away because it is thin and light.",
  "A drawing of a sunny beach has wide curved lines for the sand, a big orange circle for the sun and small blue triangles for sails. Name one line, one shape and one colour, and say what each makes you feel.",
  "Strong answer: 'The wide curved lines look smooth and relaxed. The big orange circle is warm and bright, so it feels hot and sunny. The small blue triangles look like sails on the cool water.' Each answer names the element and its effect.",
  "Thinking that an artwork is only good if it looks real. Artists choose lines, shapes and colours on purpose to create a mood or idea, so a simple, bold picture can be just as skilful as a realistic one.",
  "Choose a picture book illustration or an artwork at home. Write three sentences naming one line, one shape and one colour in it, and what each element does for the mood. Then draw a small picture of a quiet forest using only curved lines and cool colours."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w01-l2', VIS, ['thick and thin', 'a wavy line', 'a hatching line', 'a pencil grip'],
  "A drawing line can change when you change the pressure on your pencil, the speed of your hand or the way you move. Pressing lightly makes a thin, pale line and pressing harder makes a thick, dark one. Wavy lines suggest water or hair, zigzags suggest lightning or spikes, and hatching, a set of short parallel lines, can fill an area to look darker. Hold the pencil loosely, relax your shoulder and draw from your arm, not just your fingers.",
  "Example: to draw a hedgehog, Jin uses short quick zigzag lines for the spikes and lots of hatching on its back to make it darker. Then she uses a thin, light curved line for the nose and one thick dark dot for the eye. The different lines show soft and spiky parts in one picture.",
  "You want to draw a calm lake with a thundercloud above it. Choose a line type for the water and a different one for the cloud, and say how you would change the pressure for each.",
  "Strong answer: 'Long wavy lines pressed lightly for the calm water, and thick zigzag lines pressed hard for the dark cloud.' The soft pressure suggests quiet, and the hard pressure makes the cloud feel heavy and stormy.",
  "Believing that you must keep a pencil line neat and straight to be a good drawer. Experimenting with different pressures, speeds and line types is how artists find effects, and rubbing out too early stops you learning.",
  "Fill a page with a line chart: draw six boxes and fill each with a different line (thick, thin, wavy, zigzag, hatching, spiral). Then draw a rainy street using at least three of your lines, and label where you used each and what it shows."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w02-l1', VIS, ['a primary colour', 'a secondary colour', 'colour mixing', 'a tint'],
  "Red, yellow and blue are the primary colours in paint. You cannot make them by mixing other paint colours. Mixing two primaries makes a secondary colour: red and yellow make orange, yellow and blue make green, and blue and red make purple. Adding white makes a lighter tint and adding a tiny amount of a dark colour makes a darker shade. Add small amounts of the darker colour a little at a time, because it is strong.",
  "Example: Ana wants a leaf green. She puts yellow on her palette first, then adds a small dab of blue. The mix is bright green. For a darker green for shadows she adds a bit more blue. For a pale spring green she adds white. She wrote the recipe: 3 yellow + 1 blue = light green.",
  "You have only red, yellow, blue and white paint. How would you make a bright orange for a pumpkin and a pale purple for a flower? Write the colours you would mix and the amounts in parts.",
  "Strong answer: orange is red and yellow, for example 1 red + 2 yellow so it is not too red. Pale purple is blue and red mixed, then white added a little at a time. Writing the parts means you can make the same colour again.",
  "Thinking that mixing more colours always gives a better colour. Mixing all three primaries together makes a dull brown or grey, so artists mix only what they need and test on scrap paper first.",
  "Make a colour wheel with the three primary colours and three secondary colours using paint, crayons or coloured pencils. Under each secondary colour write its recipe. Then mix one tint and one shade of your favourite colour and name each."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w02-l2', VIS, ['a brush stroke', 'wet and dry', 'a mixed colour', 'a palette'],
  "When you paint, how you hold and move the brush changes the marks you make. A flat stroke with the side of the brush covers a wide area, a tip stroke makes a fine line and dabbing with a nearly dry brush makes a rough texture. Mix colours on a palette, not on your picture, and rinse the brush between colours so they stay clean. Cover your table, wear a smock and wash up carefully. Light colours go on first and dark details are added last.",
  "Example: Bo paints a sunset. He mixes orange from red and yellow, then lays a wide flat stroke across the sky. He adds a little more red at the top, blends the two while they are wet and finishes with a thin dark purple tip stroke for the hills. Light areas first, dark details last.",
  "You are painting a field of flowers under a blue sky. Plan which strokes and mixed colours you will use for the sky, the flowers and the stems, and in what order.",
  "Strong answer: 'Sky first with wide flat strokes and a pale blue made with white, then green stems with a thin tip stroke, then dabs of pink and yellow for flowers.' Large light areas come first, then details, and each colour is mixed on the palette.",
  "Thinking that painting means pressing the brush hard and adding thick paint everywhere. Too much paint hides the colours underneath and makes muddy mixes, so use light pressure and let layers dry when you want sharp edges.",
  "Paint a landscape of your choice with at least three mixed colours and three types of brush stroke. On the back write the recipe for each mixed colour and which stroke made which part. If you do not have paint, use watered-down crayon or coloured pencil."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w03-l1', VIS, ['visual texture', 'tactile texture', 'a pattern', 'repetition'],
  "Texture is how a surface feels or looks like it would feel: rough, smooth, bumpy, fluffy or prickly. Tactile texture can be touched, like the bumpy bark of a tree. Visual texture is drawn or painted to look like a feel, like dots to suggest a rough orange peel. A pattern is made when a line, shape or colour repeats in a regular way, such as stripes, spots or a repeating flower. Artists use texture and pattern to make surfaces interesting.",
  "Example: look at a zebra drawing. The black and white stripes repeat across its body, so that is a pattern. The short, soft lines drawn along its mane give the visual texture of rough hair. The artist used repeating shapes for pattern and small marks for texture. Touching a real zebra rug would give a tactile texture.",
  "A picture of a pineapple has many diamond shapes that repeat across its skin and small spiky lines at the top. Name one pattern and one visual texture in it, and say whether you could touch a tactile texture in the picture.",
  "Strong answer: 'The repeating diamonds are the pattern. The spiky lines on the leaves give a prickly visual texture. I could not touch texture in a flat picture, so it is only visual.' The answer separates pattern, visual texture and tactile texture.",
  "Mixing up pattern and texture. A pattern always repeats in a regular way, while a texture may be irregular. Something can have texture without a pattern, like a rough stone, or a pattern without texture, like stripes on smooth paper.",
  "Walk around your home or garden and find six surfaces. For each, write whether it has a pattern, a texture or both, and use a describing word. Choose two and draw a small picture of each, showing the texture with different line marks."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w03-l2', VIS, ['a rubbing', 'a texture plate', 'a design', 'repeating a motif'],
  "A rubbing captures a texture by placing thin paper over a rough surface and rubbing the side of a crayon or soft pencil across it. The raised parts print dark and the dips stay light. Hold the paper still and rub in one direction. You can cut up rubbings and arrange them, or repeat a small shape called a motif in rows to make a pattern design. Plan where each texture will go before you stick it.",
  "Example: Sia makes rubbings of a leaf, a coin and a brick wall. She cuts the leaf rubbing into small squares and repeats them in rows to make a border, with a coin circle in the centre. The repeating motif makes a border pattern and the three rubbings give three different textures.",
  "You have rubbings of a doormat, a tile and a leaf. Plan a repeating pattern design for a bookmark that uses all three. Say how you will repeat the motif and which texture goes where.",
  "Strong answer: 'I will cut the leaf into small diamonds and repeat them down both edges, put tile squares in the middle and use the doormat strip at the top and bottom.' Repeating one motif and keeping the textures in separate areas makes a clear, balanced design.",
  "Thinking that pressing hard makes a better rubbing. Pressing too hard tears the paper and blurs the texture, so rub lightly with the side of the crayon and hold the paper steady.",
  "Collect four textures from around your home and make a rubbing of each with a crayon. Cut them up and design a repeating pattern on a card, labelling the motif and the number of times it repeats. Keep all scraps in a small tray so they do not blow around."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w04-l1', VIS, ['describe', 'respond', 'an opinion with a reason', 'a title'],
  "Looking at an artwork has two steps. First describe what you can see: the subject, the colours, lines, shapes and textures. Then respond by saying what it makes you think or feel and giving a reason from the artwork. A good response uses the sentence pattern 'I think this feels calm because the colours are cool and the lines are smooth.' Everyone can have a different opinion as long as they give a reason based on what is in the work, and are polite about other opinions.",
  "Example: In a painting of a boat at night, Mia says, 'I can see a small orange boat on a dark blue sea with a pale moon. The orange is bright, so my eyes go to the boat first. It makes me feel safe because the light looks warm in the dark.' She described the colours, then responded with a feeling and a reason.",
  "Choose a picture in a book or the one on screen. Write one sentence that only describes what you see and one that responds with a feeling and a reason.",
  "Strong answer: 'I can see a tall green tree with a thick trunk and a red bird on a branch.' and 'It makes me happy because the red stands out against the green.' The first describes only, and the second gives a feeling with a reason from the artwork.",
  "Thinking that saying 'I like it' or 'it is nice' is a full response. Opinions need a reason from the artwork, such as the colour, the lines or the subject, so other people can understand what you see.",
  "Pick three different artworks from books, a gallery website with an adult or your own home. For each write a title idea, one describing sentence, one responding sentence with a reason and one question you would ask the artist. Share one with a family member and listen politely to their view."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w04-l2', VIS, ['observation', 'an outline', 'a proportion', 'looking carefully'],
  "Drawing from observation means drawing what you really see, not what you think something looks like. Look carefully at the object, then at your paper, and move your eyes back and forth. Begin with the big outline shapes and check proportions, which means how big one part is compared with another. Add details such as lines and texture after the shapes are placed. Lightly draw first so you can change things, and look at the object more than at your paper.",
  "Example: Kai draws a pear in a bowl. He sees the pear is about twice as tall as it is wide, so he draws a tall oval first. He notices a curved stem and a small bump at the bottom, adds them and then shades the dark side with light hatching lines. His pear looks real because he checked its size and shape against the real one.",
  "Draw or describe how you would draw a water bottle on a table. Which shapes would you draw first, and how would you check proportions?",
  "Strong answer: 'I would draw a long rectangle for the body and a smaller rectangle on top for the lid, check that the body is about three times taller than the lid, and add the label lines afterwards.' Big shapes first, proportions second and details last.",
  "Believing that drawing from observation means copying a photo or tracing. Observation means looking directly at a real object, noticing its shapes and sizes and showing them in your own lines.",
  "Choose three objects such as a cup, a shoe and a piece of fruit. Draw each for five minutes using big shapes first, then details. Write one sentence for each about the proportion you checked. Keep your pencil light and finish with darker lines on the edges."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w05-l1', VIS, ['a theme', 'a plan', 'a sketch', 'a place'],
  "An artwork about a place shows what makes that place special: the shapes of its buildings or plants, its colours, its textures and the feelings it gives you. Artists plan by choosing a theme, drawing small thumbnail sketches and deciding which elements will show the mood. They decide on a foreground, middle ground and background, so objects near the viewer look larger and lower on the page and far objects look smaller and higher. Choose what to leave out as well as what to include.",
  "Example: Lena makes an artwork of her local park. Her thumbnail sketches show a big gum tree in the foreground, the play equipment in the middle ground and tiny hills at the back. She chooses warm greens and yellows for a happy mood. Each shape is chosen to say 'sunny, busy park'.",
  "You want an artwork about your street. Name the place, one thing for the foreground and one for the background, and say which colours show the mood.",
  "Strong answer: 'My street: a big front gate in the foreground, houses in the middle and small trees far away. Warm yellows and soft greens make a friendly, quiet mood.' Large things in front and small things behind show distance, and the colours support the mood.",
  "Believing that you must include everything you see. Crowding in everything makes the artwork confusing, so artists choose the most important parts of the place that show its character.",
  "Plan an artwork about a place you know well. Draw three thumbnail sketches, choose one and mark the foreground, middle ground and background. Make the final artwork using at least two elements from this unit (line, shape, colour, texture or pattern), then write a short artist statement about your choices."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w05-l2', VIS, ['a showcase', 'an artist statement', 'two stars and a wish', 'a display'],
  "At the end of a unit, artists share their work and reflect on it. A showcase is a display of the best work with a label for each piece. An artist statement is two or three sentences saying what you made, which elements you used and what you want the viewer to feel. Viewers respond politely with two stars, which are two things that worked well, and a wish, which is one idea to improve. Handle other people's work with care and keep your comments kind and specific.",
  "Example: Tara's label reads: 'Rainy Day. I used thick grey zigzag lines for rain and cool blue colours to make it feel quiet. I want the viewer to feel cosy.' A visitor says, 'Star: the grey zigzag lines look like heavy rain. Star: I feel cosy because of the warm lit window. Wish: add a puddle texture.' The comments name specific elements.",
  "Write a two-sentence artist statement for one piece you made this unit, then write two stars and a wish another person could give you.",
  "Strong answer: 'I made a drawing of my street using curved lines and warm colours. I want the viewer to feel welcome.' Stars: 'The curved fence lines look friendly' and 'The yellow door stands out.' Wish: 'Add a texture to the footpath.' It names the elements, the feeling and a polite suggestion.",
  "Thinking that a wish means a criticism. A wish is a helpful idea for the next artwork, and giving specific, kind feedback helps every artist improve.",
  "Choose your best three pieces from this unit and make a mini gallery at home with a label for each. Write an artist statement for each. Invite a family member to give two stars and a wish for one piece, and write one change you would make."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w06-l1', MUS, ['a steady beat', 'a rhythm', 'a long note', 'a short note'],
  "The beat is the steady pulse in music, like a heartbeat or a clock ticking. It stays the same while the melody changes. The rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that fits over the beat. A quarter note, said 'ta', lasts one beat, a pair of eighth notes, said 'ti-ti', share one beat and a quarter rest, a silent beat, is shown with a small symbol. You can tap the beat and clap the rhythm of a song at the same time to hear the difference.",
  "Example: in 'Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star' the beat is steady taps while the words are clapped. The words 'Twin-kle twin-kle lit-tle star' have the rhythm ta ta ta ta ta ta taa (one syllable per beat, with the last one held for two beats). A song with 'ti-ti ta' would have two short sounds on one beat and then one long one. The beat never changes, but the rhythm can.",
  "Say the words 'pizza and pasta' while tapping a steady beat. Which words fall on one beat each, and which words share a beat?",
  "Strong answer: 'Piz-za and pas-ta: piz-za (ti-ti) share one beat, and (ta) takes one beat, then pas-ta (ti-ti).' So the rhythm is ti-ti ta ti-ti, over a steady beat. The beat stays even while the words make the rhythm.",
  "Thinking that the beat and the rhythm are the same thing. The beat is the steady pulse that does not change, and the rhythm is the pattern of long and short sounds that fits over it.",
  "Choose three favourite songs or rhymes. Tap the beat gently and clap the rhythm of a line from each, then write the rhythm as ta and ti-ti. Teach one rhythm to someone at home. Sit or stand, whichever is comfortable, and keep the volume low."),
L('s2-arts-y3-w06-l2', MUS, ['body percussion', 'a clap', 'a pat', 'an ostinato'],
  "Body percussion makes rhythm using your body as an instrument: clap with your hands, pat your thighs, click your fingers or stamp your feet gently. An ostinato is a short rhythm pattern that repeats over and over, such as clap, pat, pat, clap. You can play one ostinato while a friend plays a different one, which is called layering. Keep your body safe by using soft taps, no hard slaps, and stop if it hurts. You can always tap quietly or just think the pattern.",
  "Example: Priya makes a four-beat ostinato: clap, pat, pat, click. She repeats it four times. Her partner adds a second layer: stamp on beat 1 only. Together, the clap-pat-pat-click layer and the stamp layer make an interesting sound that fits the same steady beat of 60 taps a minute.",
  "Make a four-beat body percussion ostinato using two different sounds. Write it as four words and say how many times you would repeat it.",
  "Strong answer: 'Clap, clap, pat, pat. Repeat four times.' A good ostinato has a clear pattern, uses different sounds on the beat and repeats exactly the same each time so others can fit with it.",
  "Thinking that body percussion must be loud and fast to sound good. Soft, steady and accurate patterns sound clearer, and hitting hard can hurt your hands.",
  "Create two different four-beat ostinatos and write them down. Perform each one four times to a steady beat, then layer them with a partner (or record the first and play along with it). Describe how the two layers sound together."),
]

